FAQ = [
 ("Do 2015–2022 Colorado running boards fit the 2023+ Colorado?",
  "Often, yes, but only buy a part the maker lists for your year. The 2023 Colorado is a new generation with a new body, so we expected a catalog split. The fit lists we read show the opposite for steps: Westin lists its PRO TRAXX 4 (21-24010) and HDX Drop (56-14015) for the 2015–2026 Colorado Crew Cab, and Rough Country lists its HD2 boards (SRB151977) and power boards (PSR92010) for 2015–2026. That means the rocker mounting points carry over on these kits. Floor liners and tonneau covers did not carry over, so do not assume every accessory works this way. If a step listing stops at 2022, skip it."),
 ("Is the 2023+ Colorado Crew Cab only?",
  "Yes. Chevrolet sells the third-generation Colorado only as a Crew Cab with one 5 ft 2 in bed, and every pick here is a Crew Cab part. There is no Extended Cab on this generation, so there is no cab split to get wrong. The things that can still go wrong are the model year on the listing and the trim: a ZR2 has factory rocker protection that a bolt-on board would have to replace, and the snap-on ZR2 step on this page fits only that trim."),
 ("Do these steps fit the GMC Canyon?",
  "The 2023+ Canyon shares the Colorado's Crew Cab body, and most of the listings here name both trucks: Rough Country, Westin, Tyger and Hooke Road all print Colorado and Canyon in the title. The ZR2 snap-on step is the exception; its title names the Colorado ZR2, and other sellers of the same style name the Canyon AT4X. If you drive a Canyon, buy a listing that prints Canyon, or ask the seller before ordering."),
 ("Does the ZR2 come with rock rails?",
  "Chevrolet's Colorado page lists \"full skid plates and rocker protection\" for the ZR2, along with a 3 in factory lift. It does not say whether the ZR2 Bison shares the same rocker protection, so a Bison owner should check the window sticker. Factory rocker protection occupies the same area a bolt-on board would use, so on a ZR2 the clean choice is a step that clips to the factory rail, like the snap-on step on this page, rather than a replacement board. Ask the seller before you bolt anything to a ZR2 rocker."),
 ("Which trims have factory assist steps?",
  "We could not confirm this from a Chevrolet page. Chevrolet's Colorado page lists the lift and rocker protection on the ZR2 and Trail Boss, but it does not say which trims ship with assist steps. Check your window sticker, the owner's manual or the dealer build sheet. If your truck already has factory steps, an aftermarket board usually replaces them and uses the same mounting points, so confirm with the seller that the kit is sold for trucks with factory steps."),
 ("Power steps or fixed boards on a Colorado?",
  "Fixed boards cost less and have nothing to fail. Rough Country lists its HD2 aluminum board at $379.95 and its dual-motor power boards at $1,399.95. The power boards tuck under the rocker when the door closes, which keeps side clearance on a lifted Trail Boss or ZR2 and keeps the step out of the way of rocks. The trade-off is wiring, a 3 to 5 hour install per Fat Bob's Garage, and motors that can fail. If the truck is a work truck that stays on pavement, buy a fixed board; if it is a lifted truck that sees trails, the retractable step earns its price."),
 ("Nerf bar or flat running board?",
  "A nerf bar is a tube with step pads at each door. Westin's PRO TRAXX 4 uses a 4 in oval tube with 4 in wide pads. A flat board gives a continuous step the length of the cab; Rough Country's HD2 is 5 in wide per its listing and Tyger's Blade is listed at 6.6 in. Flat boards are easier for kids and older passengers and catch more mud. Nerf bars look cleaner and are easier to keep clear of snow. Both bolt to the same rocker points."),
 ("How much weight can these steps hold?",
  "Only two makers here publish a number. Fat Bob's Garage lists the Rough Country power boards at 660 lb per side. Westin does not print a load rating for the PRO TRAXX 4 or HDX Drop on the pages we read, and Rough Country's HD2 page does not list one either. Tyger, Hooke Road and the ZR2 step seller do not publish a rating on the pages we saw. If a rating matters to you, ask the seller before you buy and get it in writing."),
 ("Do I need to drill?",
  "Not on the brand-name kits. Westin says both the PRO TRAXX 4 and HDX Drop are bolt-on with no drilling on most applications and include a vehicle-specific mount kit. Fat Bob's Garage lists the Rough Country power boards as no-drill. Rough Country's HD2 listing on Amazon says bolt-on. The ZR2 snap-on step clips to the factory rail with no tools per its title. For the Hooke Road sliders and Tyger boards, read the listing's install notes, since slider-style parts sometimes bolt to the frame."),
 ("Will running boards hurt ground clearance on a Trail Boss or ZR2?",
  "A fixed board hangs below the rocker, so it lowers the side clearance of the truck and can catch on rocks. Chevrolet gives the Trail Boss a 2 in factory lift and the ZR2 a 3 in lift, which buys back some of that. For trail use, the better options are a slider-style bar built to take hits, like the Hooke Road part, a step that clips to the ZR2's factory rail, or power boards that retract under the rocker between stops. Approach and departure angles are set by the bumpers, so steps do not change them."),
]

